  <static seed="1985" source="Contrib/PCC/BasicMath/Percent/MTH20PercentApplicationType1_80.pg">
    <statement><p>Carl is paying a dinner bill of <m>{\$44.00}</m>. Carl plans to pay <m>10\%</m> in tips. How much tip will Carl pay?</p>
<p>Carl will pay <fillin characters="9" name="AnSwEr0001"/> in tip.</p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\$4.40</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><p>This problem can be boiled down to this question: What is  <m>10\%</m> of <m>44</m>? We will show multiple methods to solve this problem.</p>
<!-- PTX:WARNING: PGML wanted to make this a level 4 header. -->
<p>
Method 1</p>
<p>We will use proportion to solve this problem. Assume <m>10\%</m> of <m>44</m> is <m>x</m>, so <lq/><m>10</m> out of <m>100</m><rq/> corresponds to <lq/><m>x</m> out of <m>44</m><rq/>.</p>
<p>We will write and solve the proportion:</p>
<p><m>\displaystyle{\begin{aligned}[t]
   \frac{10}{100} \amp = \frac{x}{44} \\
   100x \amp = 10 \cdot 44 \\
   100x \amp = 440 \\
   \frac{100x}{100} \amp = \frac{440}{100} \\
   x \amp = 4.4
\end{aligned}
    }</m></p>
<p>Carl will pay <m>{\$4.40}</m> in tip.</p>
<!-- PTX:WARNING: PGML wanted to make this a level 4 header. -->
<p>
Method 2</p>
<p>We will use the percentage formula to solve this problem. This translation from English to math may help you remember the percentage formula.</p>
<p><m>2 \text{ is } 50\% \text{ of } 4 \iff 2 = 0.5 \cdot 4</m></p>
<p>The question is: What is <m>10\%</m> of <m>44</m>? Assume <m>x</m> is <m>10\%</m> of <m>44</m>. We have:</p>
<p><m>\displaystyle{ 
    \begin{aligned}
        x  \amp  = 0.1 \cdot 44 \\
        \amp  = 4.4 
        \end{aligned}
        }</m></p>
<p>Carl will pay <m>{\$4.40}</m> in tip.</p>
<!-- PTX:WARNING: PGML wanted to make this a level 4 header. -->
<p>
Method 3</p>
<p>In the sentence <lq/>What is <m>10\%</m> of <m>44</m>,<rq/></p>
<p><ul label="disc">
<li>
<p><lq/>what<rq/> is the <alert>percentage</alert>,</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>10\%</m> is the <alert>rate</alert>,</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>44</m> is the <alert>base</alert> (following the word <lq/>of<rq/>).</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
<p>By the formula <m>\text{percentage} = \text{rate} \cdot \text{base}</m>, we do a multiplication to solve the problem:</p>
<p><m>\displaystyle{ \text{percentage } = \text{rate} \cdot \text{base} = 10\% \cdot 44 = 0.1 \cdot 44 = 4.4 }</m></p>
<p>Carl will pay <m>{\$4.40}</m> in tip.</p>
</solution>
